### Keep spaces with `$raw`

Use `$raw` when the exact whitespace matters. You can still insert a value
from the surrounding scope.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    name = "Priya"
    label = [$raw:  [name]]
]

The result starts with two spaces before `Priya`.

### Write Markdown with `$md`

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    summary = [$md:Build **passed**.]
]

Moth emits a paragraph and puts `passed` in a `<strong>` element. See
@../templates/#markdown-formatting (Markdown formatting) for headings, links
and lists.

### Write markup with `$html` or display it with `$escape_html`

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    markup = [$html:<b>Build</b>]
    example = [$escape_html:<b>Build</b>]
]

In an HTML page, the first result displays **Build** in bold. The second
displays the literal text `<b>Build</b>`, including its tags.

### Check CSS with `$css`

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    rule = [$css:.notice { color: red; }]
    property = [$css "inline":color: red;]
]

The first example checks a complete rule. Use the `"inline"` operand for
declarations destined for an element's `style` attribute.

### Show source with `$code`

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    snippet = [$code "moth":greeting = [:Hello]]
]

The result highlights the Moth source. The inner template appears in the
listing with both brackets intact. Choose `$code "text"` for an unhighlighted
listing.
